import { ExplanationCopy } from "../../explanation"
import { ReaderCopy } from "../../reader-article"

pub fn copy -> ReaderCopy = ReaderCopy {
  title: "Module initialization",
  summary: (explanation ()).summary,
  purpose: [
    "When does a value imported from another file become available? Initialization evaluates the module’s top-level lets and prepares the values associated with their names.",
    "Here main.ssrg needs answer from summary.ssrg. The dependency, summary.ssrg, initializes first; main.ssrg initializes and its entry point runs afterwards. This lets callers share a computed result while keeping the start of input and output at the entry point.",
  ],
  exampleTitle: "Prepare the dependency’s value before printing from the entry point",
  reading: [
    "The package section of seseragi.toml gives the name, version and supported language versions. Under run, entry = \"main\" selects main.ssrg in the default src directory, and target = \"process\" selects a process application. Another file having a function named main does not change that selection.",
    "In summary.ssrg, fn double value: Int -> Int accepts an integer value and returns value * 2. pub let answer: Int = double 21 calls it with 21 and stores 42. pub makes answer available to another module.",
    "An Effect is a value describing work such as input or output to perform later. effect fn report returns an Effect containing output with println. The initializer report () calls that function to construct the value for pending, but does not perform the output. () is the Unit value supplied as its argument.",
    "The import in main.ssrg brings in only the public name answer. pub effect fn main exposes an entry function returning an Effect that describes output. show answer converts 42 to text, and println writes that text and a newline. The runner starts the Effect returned by this main.",
  ],
  rules: [
    "Each module initializes once, with dependencies before their consumers. This is called dependency-topological order. Importing the same module from several files does not recreate its top-level values.",
    "Within one module, lets evaluate in source order. An initializer can use earlier lets and function declarations but cannot directly read a later let. All module-level function runtime bindings are established before any of that module’s lets evaluate.",
    "Function, type, newtype, alias, struct and trait names resolve before let evaluation. Functions that call each other still require the same rec group. Functions local to a block instead become available from their declaration position; they do not use this module-level preparation rule.",
    "A function body may refer to a later let. If it is not called until after initialization, the reference waits with the function body. If an initializer calls the function immediately, every value read by it or by further functions it calls must already be initialized.",
    "Import performs the pure calculations required for initialization. It does not automatically execute stored Effects or an imported main. In the example, double 21 is evaluated, but pending’s output and main in summary.ssrg are not executed.",
  ],
  mistakes: [
    "In the early-read project below, initializing answer calls readLater (). At that point later = 42 has not evaluated. The compiler reports SES-N0201 and rejects the program before application execution. This is not an exception after startup.",
    "Trace the diagnostic from the let being initialized, through the called function, to the let it reads. Reading the let currently being initialized is also forbidden. The ordered-read project below prepares later first and then calls readLater, so it can print 42.",
  ],
  related: [
    "Check the order in which function arguments and bodies are evaluated.",
    "Check where a let or local function becomes available inside a function body.",
    "Check which main runs after initialization and which environment it receives.",
  ],
}

pub fn title -> String = (copy ()).title

pub fn loaderTitle -> String =
  "When foreign code loads, and what development tools must not do"

pub fn loader -> Array<String> = [
  "foreign declarations connect Seseragi to code in another language, such as JavaScript. There are two loading modes. A pure-load module carries the required guarantee about the purity of its load-time evaluation. Under that guarantee, the runtime may evaluate it once during Seseragi module initialization.",
  "A task-load module is not evaluated just because its Seseragi module is imported. Loading waits for the first foreign Task execution. Task is an alias for an Effect with no required services or recoverable failure. Merely storing an Effect does not start that execution.",
  "The parser, type checker, formatter, language server and document generator must never evaluate foreign runtime code. pure-load does not authorize development tools to run it. An initialization-order diagnostic is not repaired by looking up missing services or values by name at runtime.",
]

pub fn explanation -> ExplanationCopy = ExplanationCopy {
  question: "When is an imported value calculated, and when does output start?",
  summary: "Dependencies initialize once before their consumers, with lets evaluated from top to bottom in each file. Constructing an Effect is separate from executing its input or output.",
  reading: "main.ssrg imports answer from summary.ssrg. Initialization first calculates double 21 to prepare 42 and stores the Effect returned by report () in pending. The runner then executes the entry point in the configured main.ssrg.",
  result: "The output is only 42 and a newline. Neither the message in summary.ssrg’s main nor the message stored in pending appears. Pure function calls can run during initialization; executing an Effect waits for the entry point to start it.",
}
